Subject: [PATCH] feat: Q4 firm-wide capacity forecasting (rolling 12-month
 view)

Extends the existing point-in-time capacity/allocation dashboard
(/reports/dashboard/capacity) into a forward-looking rolling forecast:

Backend:
- User.standard_weekly_hours (default 40, overridable) so allocation
  percentages can be turned into actual hour figures -- needed for any
  meaningful over/under-booking signal beyond raw percentages.
- app/core/capacity_forecast.py: for each active staff member and each
  of the next N calendar months, computes capacity hours (net of
  approved leave), allocated hours/percent from individual
  ProjectAssignment rows on planning/active engagements, and a
  bench/full/overbooked status. Department-wide assignments are
  intentionally excluded from the numeric load -- consistent with how
  allocation_percent is already rejected on those at creation.
- GET /capacity/forecast: per-person monthly breakdown, filterable by
  department_id, user_id, months (1-24), and start_date.
- GET /capacity/forecast/summary: firm-wide (or department-scoped)
  rollup of overbooked/bench headcount per forecasted month -- the
  busy-season staffing risk and hiring-signal view.
- PATCH /users/{email}/weekly-hours (admin-only): sets the baseline
  used above; without this there was no way to mark someone part-time
  for forecasting purposes.
- Migration b0a3248de0db, verified via upgrade/downgrade/re-upgrade
  round-trip against both the incremental chain and a
  create_all()+stamp-head dev DB.
- tests/test_capacity_forecast.py (18 tests): allocation-to-hours math,
  open-ended engagements carrying forward, overbooked/bench thresholds,
  completed/cancelled engagements excluded, department-only assignments
  excluded, approved leave reducing capacity hours, department/user
  filters, months range validation (1-24), auth requirement.
- Full suite: 485/485 passing (467 existing + 18 new), no regressions.

Frontend:
- New "Capacity Forecast" tab on the Reports dashboard, alongside the
  existing point-in-time Capacity tab: department/month-range filters,
  an overbooked-months-in-horizon summary stat, and an expandable
  per-person month-by-month table (allocated %, capacity/leave/available
  hours, status, engagement names).
- next build compiles cleanly with no new errors.

Caveat: weekly-hours is settable via API but not yet exposed in the
Users page UI, matching how standard_billing_rate is handled today
(also API-only, no UI control).
